+// tailscale_rebind makes netmon re-read the network as a route change would,
+// then re-binds the node's UDP sockets, pings its DERP connections
+// (reconnecting one that does not answer within 3 s), forgets the peers'
+// direct paths and re-discovers its endpoints. Nothing does this on its own
+// on iOS: netmon has no time-jump check there, and its route socket stops
+// reporting changes once the process has been suspended. Without it, sockets
+// stay bound to an interface the phone left, and a NAT mapping or DERP
+// connection that died while the process was suspended lasts until DERP's
+// 120 s read deadline.
+//
+// default_if, when neither NULL nor empty, names the interface the OS routes
+// through (from NWPathMonitor). On Apple platforms new sockets bind to it
+// rather than to netmon's view, which can be stale; elsewhere it is ignored.
+//
+// Returns:
+// 	0     - success
+// 	EBADF - sd is not a valid tailscale
+// 	-1    - call tailscale_errmsg for details
+extern int tailscale_rebind(tailscale sd, const char* default_if);
